\title{Record types}
\import{prelude}

\p{Record types compose multiple types into a single type, whose elements consist of elements of each of the individual pieces. A record type declaration consists of a list of \em{fields}, each with its own type. A record is formed by a \code{\kw{sig} ... \kw{end}} block, with one field per line. For instance:}

\pre{%
\kw{theory} PersonDetails := \kw{sig}
  name : \kwi{String}
  age : \kwi{Int}
\kw{end}
}

\p{An instance of \code{PersonDetails} stores \em{one person}'s name and age. The two fields become columns in a single table:}

\dbexample{
  \p{\strong{Table \code{root}}}

  \table{
    \row{\hcell{\code{a.name : \kwi{String}}} \hcell{\code{a.age : \kwi{Int}}}}
  }{
    \row{\cell{\code{"Wonka"}} \cell{38}}
  }

  \p{\strong{Constraints:}}
  \ul{
    \li{\code{root} has exactly one row.}
  }
}

\p{Fields can also be collections, by giving them the type \code{\kwi{Set}}:}

\pre{%
\kw{theory} Tour := \kw{sig}
  Child : \kwi{Set}
  Adult : \kwi{Set}
\kw{end}
}

\p{Elements of \code{Tour} consist of a pair of a collection of children and a collection of adults.}

\dbexample{
  \p{\strong{Table \code{root.Child}}}

  \table{
    \row{\hcell{Row ID}}
  }{
    \row{\cell{\code{c1}}}
  }

  \p{\strong{Table \code{root.Adult}}}

  \table{
    \row{\hcell{Row ID}}
  }{
    \row{\cell{\code{a1}}}
    \row{\cell{\code{a2}}}
  }

  \p{\strong{Constraints:} None.}
}

\p{Each element of a collection is represented by a row with its own opaque row-id.}

\p{We have not yet explained how to introduce elements of a record ty
pe \em{inside} a Coln file, rather than by interacting with the theory as a database. This we leave to [[001K]], where we interpret such elements as "translations" of one theory into another.}
